      "text": "To simplify the expression \\(\\sqrt[4]{2^7 \\cdot 3^3}\\), we start by expressing the fourth root in terms of exponents. We can rewrite the expression as:\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt[4]{2^7 \\cdot 3^3} = (2^7 \\cdot 3^3)^{1/4}\n\\]\n\nUsing the property of exponents \\((x^m \\cdot y^n)^p = x^{mp} \\cdot y^{np}\\), we get:\n\n\\[\n(2^7 \\cdot 3^3)^{1/4} = 2^{7/4} \\cdot 3^{3/4}\n\\]\n\nThis can be rewritten as:\n\n\\[\n2^{7/4} = 2^{1 + 3/4} = 2^1 \\cdot 2^{3/4} = 2 \\cdot 2^{3/4}\n\\]\n\nSo the expression becomes:\n\n\\[\n2 \\cdot 2^{3/4} \\cdot 3^{3/4} = 2 \\cdot (2 \\cdot 3)^{3/4} = 2 \\cdot 6^{3/4}\n\\]\n\nWe can also express \\(6^{3/4}\\) as \\((2 \\cdot 3)^{3/4} = 2^{3/4} \\cdot 3^{3/4}\\). However, the problem asks for the simplified form \\(a \\sqrt[4]{b}\\), so we can directly write:\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt[4]{2^7 \\cdot 3^3} = 2 \\cdot 3^{3/4} \\cdot 2^{1/4} = 2 \\cdot \\sqrt[4]{2^3 \\cdot 3^3} = 2 \\cdot 3^{3/4} \\cdot \\sqrt[4]{2^3} = 2 \\cdot 3^{3/4} \\cdot 2^{3/4} = 2 \\cdot 6^{3/4}\n\\]\n\nThus, we have \\(a = 2\\) and \\(b = 27\\), so \\(a + b = 2 + 27 = 29\\).\n\nThe final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{29}\n\\]",
