    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to simplify the expression \\(\\sqrt[4]{2^7 \\cdot 3^3}\\) to the form \\(a\\sqrt[4]{b}\\), where \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are positive integers, and then find \\(a + b\\).\n\nFirst, let's express the given expression in a more manageable form:\n\\[\n\\sqrt[4]{2^7 \\cdot 3^3}\n\\]\n\nWe start by using the property of exponents that states \\(\\sqrt[n]{a^m} = a^{m/n}\\). Applying this property, we get:\n\\[\n\\sqrt[4]{2^7 \\cdot 3^3} = (2^7 \\cdot 3^3)^{1/4}\n\\]\n\nNext, we apply the power of a product rule \\((a \\cdot b)^n = a^n \\cdot b^n\\):\n\\[\n(2^7 \\cdot 3^3)^{1/4} = 2^{7/4} \\cdot 3^{3/4}\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to express these exponents in a form that can be simplified further. Recall that \\(a^{m/n} = (a^m)^{1/n}\\). Therefore:\n\\[\n2^{7/4} = (2^7)^{1/4}\n\\]\n\\[\n3^{3/4} = (3^3)^{1/4}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(2^7 = 128\\), we have:\n\\[\n2^{7/4} = 128^{1/4}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly, since \\(3^3 = 27\\), we have:\n\\[\n3^{3/4} = 27^{1/4}\n\\]\n\nThus, the expression becomes:\n\\[\n2^{7/4} \\cdot 3^{3/4} = 128^{1/4} \\cdot 27^{1/4}\n\\]\n\nWe can combine the exponents since they have the same base:\n\\[\n128^{1/4} \\cdot 27^{1/4} = (128 \\cdot 27)^{1/4}\n\\]\n\nNow, we calculate \\(128 \\cdot 27\\):\n\\[\n128 \\cdot 27 = 3456\n\\]\n\nSo, we have:\n\\[\n(128 \\cdot 27)^{1/4} = 3456^{1/4}\n\\]\n\nThis is in the form \\(a\\sqrt[4]{b}\\), where \\(a = 3456^{1/4}\\) and \\(b = 1\\). Therefore:\n\\[\na = 3456^{1/4}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(3456 = 6^5\\), we have:\n\\[\n3456^{1/4} = (6^5)^{1/4} = 6^{5/4}\n\\]\n\nThus, \\(a = 6^{5/4}\\) and \\(b = 1\\). Therefore, \\(a + b = 6^{5/4} + 1\\).\n\nHowever, we need to simplify \\(a\\) to a more recognizable form. Notice that:\n\\[\n6^{5/4} = 6^{1 + 1/4} = 6 \\cdot 6^{1/4}\n\\]\n\nThus, we have:\n\\[\na + b = 6 + 1 = 7\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{7}\n\\]",
